$DIR/main.p:17:10: the field hits of a synced[Stats] is read here without holding mu, which guards it
    Every write to the field hits of a synced[Stats] that another task may run
    at the same time holds mu: main.p:11 in record (the task started by task
    record at main.p:24). So the compiler infers that mu guards the field hits
    of a synced[Stats], and every read must hold it too: a read without it may
    run while a write is half done.
    Here the task started by task peek at main.p:25 reads it at main.p:17 in
    peek with no lock held.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold the lock while reading:
        mu.Lock()
        v := the field hits of a synced[Stats]
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3503" for a longer explanation)
